## Purpose of this doc

This project is a **.NET MAUI** app (Android/iOS/Windows) that helps users **wake up / build a daily routine** by automatically playing Jehovah’s Witnesses (JW) media (Bible readings, music, dramas, videos) at scheduled times.

## Overall goal of the app (what it’s used for)

- **Primary goal**: behave like an alarm clock, but instead of (or before) a ringtone it plays selected JW.org content (often Bible chapters).
- **Typical use**:
  - configure one or more recurring schedules (time + days)
  - choose what to play (language, publication, section, track; optionally “begin with music”)
  - at alarm time, the app triggers playback (and can pre-download media so playback starts reliably)
  - supports car experiences (Android Auto / CarPlay) and system media controls for starting/controlling playback
- **Design constraints**:
  - alarms must work even when the app is not running (OS notifications/alarms + background bootstrap)
  - playback should survive platform lifecycle constraints (foreground services on Android, background audio on iOS)
  - large media catalogs must be discoverable without shipping full audio (media index DB + ad-hoc cataloging + caching)

This file is meant to give Cursor **reliable navigation anchors**:
- where the app starts and bootstraps
- where data lives (two SQLite DBs)
- where side-effects happen (Fluxor Effects)
- how alarms trigger playback (platform services)
- how the media index is built/updated (cataloger tool + embedded resource)

